<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec_rel-em" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  	<title>Relativistic Electromagnetism</title>

	<assemblage component="implementation">
  <title>Implementation Notes</title>
  <p>
	The activities in this section were designed to be implemented in a 1-hour interactive classroom, after students have completed the rest of the chapter.
	</p>
  </assemblage>



<activity>
	<title>Warm-up</title>
	<statement>
		<p></p>
	<p>
		The figure shows the same situation in two different inertial reference frames.
	</p>
	<figure xml:id="fig_EM-Ref3">
			<title>Reference Frames Figure 3</title>
			<caption>A charge and its field in two different reference frames.</caption>
			<image source="EM-Ref3.png" width = "50%"><description><p>Above: a stationary point charge creates an electric field at a point a distance r above it.  Below: the same point charge, now moving to the left in a moving reference frame, also makes a magnetic field into the screen.</p></description></image>
			</figure>
	<p>
		In each frame, write equations for the electric and magnetic fields at the marked point.
	</p>
</statement>
</activity>

<activity>
	<title>Warm-up</title>
	<statement>
		<p></p>
	<p>
		The figure at right shows the same situation in two different inertial reference frames.
	</p>
	<figure xml:id="fig_EM-Ref4">
			<title>Reference Frames Figure 4</title>
			<caption>Two charges in two different reference frames.</caption>
			<image source="EM-Ref4.png" width = "50%"><description><p>Above: two vertically separated point charges move with speed v to the right.  Below: the same point charges are now stationary in a frame that moves to the right.</p></description></image>
			</figure>
	<p>
		In each frame, write equations for the electric and magnetic fields at the marked point.
	</p>
	<p>
		Compare the magnitudes of the forces between the charges in the two reference frames.
	</p>
</statement>
</activity>

	
	

<!--NO AI-->
</section>

